concepts/trust-model#unifying-trust-point","content":"Unifying trust point"},"2092":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#bridge-ca","content":"Bridge CA"},"2093":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-lists","content":"Trust lists"},"2094":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#what-lamassu-can-demonstrate","content":"What Lamassu can demonstrate"},"2095":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"Identity flow"},"2096":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"Enrollment, validation and distribution CAs"},"2097":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"Validation and revocation"},"2098":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#custody-limits","content":"Custody limits"},"2099":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Recommended pattern"},"2100":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","content":"In Lamassu, trust does not simply mean that a certificate exists in the inventory. A consumer trusts an identity when it can build a chain to a root it recognizes, check that the certificate is fit for the intended use and verify that it is still valid."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#who-can-issue","content":"The CA hierarchy defines which authorities can sign. A root CA establishes the anchor of trust; intermediate CAs separate operating domains and avoid using the root for every issuance."},"2102":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#what-can-be-issued","content":"Certificate profiles limit validity, subject, uses, extensions and cryptographic parameters. Possessing a CA does not replace an issuance policy."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#who-can-request-it","content":"The DMS acts as the enrollment point. It can authenticate an EST request with a client certificate, an external webhook or a combination of both. CAs configured to validate the requester do not have to be the CA that will issue the new identity."},"2104":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#who-accepts-the-result","content":"The consumer keeps its own trust store. EST `/cacerts` can distribute the service TLS certificate, the enrollment CA and selected managed CAs. Initial trust for authenticating that service must come from an already trusted channel. Download and inspect the contents: do not assume they form a complete chain or install every entry as a root."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"RFC 5217 distinguishes a PKI's internal structure from relationships between domains. Below, `A → B` means A signs a CA certificate for B. Validation starts at the relying party's chosen anchor; a signature in one direction does not imply reciprocal trust."},"2106":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Model"},"2107":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Structure"},"2108":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Considerations"},"2109":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Single CA"},"2110":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"`CA → identity`."},"2111":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"The root issues identities directly; its compromise affects all of them."},"2112":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Hierarchical"},"2113":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-32","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"`Root → intermediate → identity`."},"2114":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-33","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Distribute the root and present intermediates; constrain depth, uses and validity."},"2115":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-34","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Full or partial mesh"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-35","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Self-signed CAs cross-certify each other; a partial mesh has missing links."},"2117":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-36","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Check each direction and anchor: some domains may be unreachable in a partial mesh."},"2118":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-37","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Hybrid"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-38","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Combines hierarchies and cross-certification links."},"2120":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-39","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Document alternative paths and constraints; consumers may build or accept different paths."},"2121":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-40","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Unifying trust point"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-41","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"`Unifying CA → PCA-A` and `Unifying CA → PCA-B`."},"2123":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-42","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Cross-domain validation requires trust in the unifying CA and access to cross-certificates. PCAs retain their self-signed certificates."},"2124":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-43","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Direct cross-certification"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-44","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"`PCA-A → PCA-B`; add `PCA-B → PCA-A` for reciprocity."},"2126":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-45","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Each domain keeps its anchor; test both directions and restrict unwanted paths."},"2127":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-46","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Bridge CA"},"2128":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-47","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"`PCA-A → Bridge → PCA-B`; add reverse signatures for the opposite path."},"2129":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-48","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"The bridge is not a domain anchor and issues end-entity certificates only for its own operation. Agree policies and constraints."},"2130":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-49","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Trust list"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-50","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"The consumer trusts roots A and B, locally or through a trust authority managing the list."},"2132":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-51","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Cross-certification is unnecessary. Distributing certificates does not install trust anchors; control updates and removal."},"2133":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-52","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"A PCA is the principal CA designated for relationships between PKIs; an identity is an end-entity certificate. The first four rows describe architectures within a PKI, the next three relationships between domains, and the last a trust model external to those relationships. An ordinary intermediate alone does not demonstrate a unifying CA."},"2134":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-53","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-architectures-and-interoperability","content":"Each diagram below is a short sequence of steps you can play, pause or jump between. A CA is drawn as what it is — a key pair — with one row per certificate issued for that key: a root starts with its self-signed certificate and gains a second row when another CA cross-signs it, same subject and same key under a different issuer. Rows a later step will fill are outlined from the start. Solid arrows indicate certificate issuance; dashed arrows indicate configured trust or distribution, as labeled. The green trace is a validation path: it runs from the identity back to the anchor, opposite to the issuance arrows. An available path is not necessarily accepted by a validator."},"2135":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-54","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"A single CA signs identities directly:"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-55","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"A hierarchy separates the root from issuing CAs:"},"2137":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-56","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"A single-CA PKI uses a self-signed root to issue identities directly. It is easy to understand and useful for an initial test, but concentrates issuance and trust in one key. If that key is compromised, the anchor must be replaced and affected identities renewed."},"2138":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-57","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"In a hierarchy, the root signs one or more intermediate CAs that issue identities. This separates, for example, production from testing or devices from services, and allows root usage to be restricted. Each consumer needs to trust the root and have the intermediates available to build the chain. The design must account for permitted depth, uses and expiration at each CA."},"2139":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-58","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"In Lamassu, create a root and issue an identity for the first case; for the second, create an intermediate under another root and issue from it. Download the certificates and validate both chains from a consumer, including a test with an incorrect anchor."},"2140":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-59","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#single-ca-and-hierarchy","content":"The following interactive walkthrough shows both scenarios in the console: the standalone root and the hierarchy, with the public certificates and the downloaded identity certificates."},"2141":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-60","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"Bilateral cross-certification: each domain keeps its anchor. Validating B from A uses B’s certificate signed by A; the reverse direction requires the opposite signature."},"2142":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-61","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"Example partial mesh: the C → A and C → B signatures are absent; reciprocity must not be assumed."},"2143":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-62","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"Cross-certification lets one CA issue a certificate for another CA's public key. A consumer trusting A can then build a path to an identity issued by B using B's certificate signed by A. B's self-signed certificate can remain available for its own consumers. For consumers trusting B to validate identities from A, a signature in the opposite direction and a path accepted by their validator are needed."},"2144":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-63","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"A mesh extends this relationship to several CAs: it is full when every CA signs every other CA, and partial when some links are missing. A full mesh offers direct links but increases the number of relationships to manage; a partial mesh reduces links but may leave paths missing or harder to build. A path shown in a graph is not necessarily acceptable to a validator."},"2145":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-64","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"To demonstrate this, create roots A, B and C and test certificates. Start with A → B, validate an identity from B using A as the anchor, and check that the opposite direction is not covered until B → A is added. Add links to build a partial mesh and compare available paths. Control the certificates supplied to the validator so alternative paths can be identified."},"2146":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-65","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#direct-cross-certification-and-mesh","content":"The following walkthrough shows a partial mesh with A ↔ B, A → C and B → C. The certificates were prepared before recording; the guide describes validations performed outside the console."},"2147":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-66","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"Two hierarchies keep their roots and A signs CA B. From anchor A, the chain to identity B includes B’s cross-certificate and intermediate B."},"2148":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-67","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"A hybrid PKI combines hierarchies of subordinate CAs with cross-certification. It can arise when two organizations with their own hierarchies need to interoperate without replacing their roots. Each branch retains its structure, but paths between them make certificate distribution, revocation and policy interpretation more complex."},"2149":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-68","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"To demonstrate it, create roots A and B, an issuing intermediate under B and an identity issued by it, and add A → B. From anchor A, the consumer needs B's certificate signed by A and B's intermediate to validate the final identity. Also check failure when a link is missing and look for alternative paths."},"2150":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-69","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"The following walkthrough starts from those CAs, prepared before recording, and performs the A → B cross-signing with the **Cross Sign** operation. It then compares the authorities table, hierarchy and graph: B appears twice, as a self-signed root and under A, with the same key and a different issuer. Each step's guide describes the validations, which were performed outside the console."},"2151":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-70","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"With OpenSSL, using only A as the anchor and B's cross certificate plus the intermediate as auxiliary material, the path `identity → intermediate → B (cross) → A` validates:"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-71","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#hybrid-model","content":"Validation fails if the cross certificate is omitted, if the intermediate is missing, or if an unrelated root is used as the anchor. If the validator also trusts B, it may choose the direct path, shorter and not going through A: the cross certificate does not make it unnecessary. The cross certificate has its own expiry (three years in the demo), earlier than root B's and the intermediate's, so the path through A stops validating the intermediate before the direct one does. The demo's certificates contain no certificate policies, name constraints, policy constraints or maximum path length, and this version's **Cross Sign** operation offers no controls for them. The demo therefore does not prove conformance with RFC 5217, only path building with the basic extensions."},"2153":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-72","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#unifying-trust-point","content":"Relying parties validating both domains use the unifying CA as their anchor and the PCAs’ cross-certificates."},"2154":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-73","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#unifying-trust-point","content":"This model adds a superior CA that unilaterally signs the PCAs of participating domains. Each PCA can keep its self-signed certificate and existing operation; consumers that need cross-domain validation use the unifying CA as their anchor and obtain the required cross-certificates."},"2155":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-74","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#unifying-trust-point","content":"The benefit is a common trust point without necessarily rebuilding existing PKIs. In exchange, participants must distribute and govern the new anchor, agree policies and assess the impact of its compromise or replacement. A consumer needing only its own domain can continue to trust its existing PCA."},"2156":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-75","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#unifying-trust-point","content":"For the demo, first create two self-signed PCAs, then the links Unifier → PCA-A and Unifier → PCA-B. Issue an identity from each domain and validate both from the unifying anchor; check that the cross-certificates correspond to the same keys as the original PCAs."},"2157":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-76","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#bridge-ca","content":"Each domain keeps its anchor. Signatures in both directions allow paths A → bridge → B and B → bridge → A."},"2158":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-77","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#bridge-ca","content":"A bridge CA connects domains that retain their own anchors. To validate an identity from B using A as the anchor, the path can follow A → Bridge → B; the opposite direction requires the corresponding links. The bridge must not become a domain anchor and does not issue end-entity certificates except those required for its own operation."},"2159":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-78","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#bridge-ca","content":"Centralizing relationships through a bridge can reduce direct agreements, but requires shared governance, documented policies, policy mapping and consistent constraints. It also increases the importance of validators discovering and processing paths and extensions correctly."},"2160":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-79","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#bridge-ca","content":"For the demo, create two PCAs and a bridge CA, establish the links needed in both directions, and validate an identity from each domain using the other's anchor. Inspect the extensions and document any RFC requirement that the deployed version or validator cannot verify: a connected graph alone does not demonstrate a complete bridge model."},"2161":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-80","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-lists","content":"Local list: the relying party configures both roots as anchors, without cross-signatures between them."},"2162":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-81","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-lists","content":"Trust authority: an entity maintains a shared list that relying parties use according to their configuration."},"2163":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-82","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-lists","content":"A trust list allows a consumer to accept several PKIs by explicitly installing their anchors, without the CAs cross-certifying one another. The list may be managed locally or by a trust authority for multiple consumers. This is a direct approach, although managing additions, removals and updates becomes harder as the number of consumers grows."},"2164":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-83","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#trust-lists","content":"For the demo, create two independent roots and an identity under each. Validate both with a list containing both roots, then remove one root and check that the corresponding identity no longer validates. If you distribute certificates through EST `/cacerts`, show the consumer's decision to install them as anchors separately."},"2165":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-84","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#what-lamassu-can-demonstrate","content":"You can create roots and intermediates, issue identities and register external CAs as described in Authorities. The dashboard code also includes a cross-signing operation that creates another certificate for the target CA's existing key, signed by the selected CA. Availability depends on the deployed version. In that flow both CAs must be active and have their private keys in the KMS; a certificate-only import is insufficient. Verify the public key and that the cross-certificate subject reproduces the target CA's subject: some imported subjects cannot be reproduced exactly."},"2166":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-85","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#what-lamassu-can-demonstrate","content":"The operation can build links for these topologies, but the graph or issuance alone does not prove interoperability or full RFC conformance. Inspect `basicConstraints`, `keyCertSign`, validity, path length, and name and policy constraints. Verify `policyMappings` and `policyConstraints` availability and processing in the deployed version and each validator. Agree policies, assurance levels, publication and revocation across domains."},"2167":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-86","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#what-lamassu-can-demonstrate","content":"For each path, download the identity and required CA certificates. Validate from the relying party with its explicit trust anchor and intermediates as auxiliary material; also test the reverse direction and a case that must fail. Repeat with the revocation and policy checks actually enforced by that consumer. See Validation and Trust distribution."},"2168":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-87","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"The device proves it can enroll according to the DMS policy."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-88","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"The DMS validates the request and forwards the CSR to the enrollment CA."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-89","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"In the device-generated CSR flow, the CA applies the profile and signs without receiving the device’s private key."},"2171":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-90","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"The device installs the certificate and the chain it needs."},"2172":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-91","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"The system that trusts the device validates the chain, dates, uses and revocation status."},"2173":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-92","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#identity-flow","content":"OIDC or X.509 identifies the operator calling Lamassu. The authorization service decides which actions it may perform. The X.509 hierarchy, on the other hand, determines whether a certificate presented by a device is trustworthy."},"2174":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-93","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"A DMS relates three sets that are worth designing separately:"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-94","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"**Enrollment CA:** signs the new identities."},"2176":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-95","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"**Validation CAs:** authenticate the client certificates used for enrollment or migration. During re-enrollment, the enrollment CA is always trusted and Lamassu then tries the validation CAs configured in the re-enrollment block; those are optional."},"2177":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-96","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"**Distributed CAs:** form the content the device obtains through EST `/cacerts`. You can include the service TLS certificate, the enrollment CA and an explicit list of managed CAs. Including the issuer does not automatically add all its ancestors."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-97","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#enrollment-validation-and-distribution-cas","content":"This separation makes it possible to migrate a fleet: you temporarily accept certificates from the old hierarchy, issue with the new one and distribute both chains during the overlap."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-98","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"A valid chain does not by itself guarantee that a certificate should be accepted. The consumer must also check:"},"2180":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-99","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"that the current date is between `Not Before` and `Not After`;"},"2181":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-100","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"that `Key Usage` and `Extended Key Usage` allow the operation;"},"2182":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-101","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"that names, SANs and subject match the expected identity;"},"2183":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-102","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"that the certificate does not appear revoked in OCSP or a CRL."},"2184":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-103","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"An OCSP `Good` status does not replace chain, date, usage and identity checks either; Validation brings those criteria together."},"2185":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-104","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#validation-and-revocation","content":"During the mTLS authentication of a DMS, Lamassu attempts to check revocation. If the certificate does not contain enough information to perform that check, the backend logs a warning and continues treating it as not revoked. Design your profiles and distribution points to avoid that situation in production."},"2186":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-105","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#custody-limits","content":"The KMS keeps the reference to the key and delegates the operation to the configured engine. With an HSM or a cloud KMS, the private key can remain non-exportable. That protects custody, but it does not by itself prevent misuse: you still must restrict who can request a signature and audit those operations."},"2187":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-106","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Keep the root out of daily operations, or with very restricted access."},"2188":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-107","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Issue from intermediate CAs separated by environment or risk domain."},"2189":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-108","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Assign explicit profiles to every use case."},"2190":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-109","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Distribute the new trust before starting a rotation."},"2191":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-110","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Keep enough overlap to renew a disconnected fleet."},"2192":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-111","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Validate from the consuming system, not only from the Lamassu console."},"2193":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model-112","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/concepts/trust-model#recommended-pattern","content":"Continue with CA hierarchy and rotation to turn this model into an operating procedure."},"2194":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"page","content":"Revocation lists (CRL)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI","Validation"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","content":"Generate and distribute signed lists to validate certificates offline."},"2196":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"CRL settings"},"2197":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#cas-that-share-a-key","content":"CAs that share a key"},"2198":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"Periodic and automatic regeneration"},"2199":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-contents","content":"CRL contents"},"2200":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"Public download endpoint"},"2201":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"CRL settings API"},"2202":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Common CRL settings errors"},"2203":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#from-the-interface","content":"From the interface"},"2204":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"References"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","content":"A CRL is an issuer-signed list of revoked certificates. This guide is for an operator publishing lists and an integrator using them locally. You need the issuing CA certificate, its CA ID and access to the published endpoint. The client must maintain a current copy: offline operation still requires refreshing evidence."},"2206":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"The CRL settings of a CA store the configuration and metadata of its latest published list. Each CA has its own settings, keyed by its CA ID — even when two CAs share a key: they share the SKI, not the CRL. Generation requires a usable issuer key; do not assume an imported CA without a key has an available CRL."},"2207":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Field"},"2208":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Purpose"},"2209":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`ca_id`"},"2210":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"ID of the CA the settings belong to."},"2211":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`status`"},"2212":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`ACTIVE` settings generate CRLs. `ARCHIVED` ones belong to a CA that no longer exists: their last CRL is still served, but never regenerated."},"2213":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`legacy_ski`"},"2214":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Read-only. SKI the settings were keyed by before the 4.0.0 migration; only migrated settings have it, and `/crl/<ski>` keeps resolving to them."},"2215":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`latest_crl`"},"2216":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Version, validity start and end of the last published list. Read-only."},"2217":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`crl_options` field"},"2218":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Purpose"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Initial value"},"2220":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`validity`"},"2221":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Time from `thisUpdate` to `nextUpdate`."},"2222":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"7 days."},"2223":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`refresh_interval`"},"2224":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Time since the issuance of the latest published CRL that triggers scheduled renewal; `0` disables only this trigger. The effective granularity is bounded by the job frequency (see Periodic and automatic regeneration)."},"2225":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"6 days and 23 hours."},"2226":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`regenerate_on_revoke`"},"2227":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-32","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Request regeneration on revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` events."},"2228":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-33","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`true`."},"2229":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-34","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`signer_serial_number`"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-35","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Serial number of the existing certificate that signs the CRL."},"2231":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-36","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Serial number of the CA’s current certificate."},"2232":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-37","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"The signer must be an existing certificate with the `cRLSign` key usage and the same subject and public key as the CA. Certificates from before a reissue qualify. A delegated signer is not supported: a certificate with the CA’s subject but a different key is rejected."},"2233":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-38","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"When a CA is reissued and the signer is the superseded CA certificate, the VA moves the signer to the new certificate — provided it carries `cRLSign` — and republishes the CRL. If the new certificate lacks `cRLSign`, the VA keeps the previous signer and logs a warning. If the signer is any other certificate with the CA’s subject and key, it is deliberately left unchanged; the previous certificate then cannot be deleted while the settings use it (the API returns `409` `certificate is in use`), and to retire it you update the signer with `PUT`."},"2234":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-39","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"A CA whose signer lacks `cRLSign` cannot generate its CRL, and an update that selects a signer without `cRLSign` is rejected (400 error). Since 4.0 the VA requires this usage. Check before importing with `openssl x509 -noout -ext keyUsage` and, if it is missing, obtain a certificate for the same CA and key that includes it and update the settings to use it as the signer. After reissuing a CA, also check the key usage of the new certificate: it may not keep the original’s usages, and without `cRLSign` the settings keep their previous signer."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-40","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Deleting a CA keeps its CRL settings, and they cannot be removed through the API. What happens next depends on when the CA was deleted:"},"2236":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-41","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"The 4.0.0 upgrade migration archives the settings of the CAs that had been deleted before the upgrade. Their `status` is `ARCHIVED` and their `ca_id` is `legacy-<ski>`: the last CRL keeps being served, but it is never regenerated, any `PUT` returns 409, and there is no API way back to `ACTIVE`."},"2237":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-42","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"A CA deleted while running 4.0.0 leaves `ACTIVE` settings. The last CRL keeps being served, but once it expires nothing regenerates it: the renewal job logs `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ca-id>` on every cycle because it can no longer resolve the CA. The options can still be edited while the signer is unchanged; changing the signer fails, because there is no CA left to match it."},"2238":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-43","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Adjust validity and signer before deleting a CA."},"2239":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-44","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"`PUT` validates the signer only when the request changes it. If the stored signer is no longer valid, the other options can still be edited, but the CRL cannot be regenerated until a valid signer is set."},"2240":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-45","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"Sharing a key between CAs is a migration blocker: the 4.0.0 upgrade aborts while an SKI belongs to more than one CA. See CAs that share a key."},"2241":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-46","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings","content":"CRL versions are kept as PEM in object storage at `pki/va/crl/<ca-id>/<version>.crl`; the public endpoint returns DER. CRLs published before the 4.0.0 upgrade remain at `pki/va/crl/<ski>/<version>.crl` and keep being served."},"2242":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-47","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#cas-that-share-a-key","content":"Since 4.0.0, each CA publishes its own CRL even when its key is shared: two CAs with the same key have the same SKI, but each has its own CRL settings and its own list, signed by a certificate with that CA’s identity."},"2243":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-48","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#cas-that-share-a-key","content":"Certificates issued from now on carry `/crl/<ca-id>` distribution points, so each CA’s certificates resolve to its own list. Certificates issued before the upgrade keep their `/crl/<ski>` distribution points: that URL keeps resolving to the settings the upgrade migrated, and always serves the list of the CA that owned that SKI when the upgrade ran. It is never rebound to a CA created later with the same key. After regeneration, the migrated list includes both URLs in its *Issuing Distribution Point* (OID `2.5.29.28`), so a verifier that compares the IDP with the CDP of the certificate accepts either form. Until the VA scheduled CRL job successfully regenerates it, the pre-upgrade list contains only the legacy `/crl/<ski>` IDP; IDP-checking verifiers cannot validate newly issued certificates with `/crl/<ca-id>` CDPs. The first run can take up to about an hour with the default schedule, and failures can extend the window."},"2244":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-49","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#cas-that-share-a-key","content":"Sharing an SKI between CAs blocks the upgrade: the migration aborts and names the SKIs. To detect the problem before upgrading, compare the SKI of the CAs in the inventory: two with the same SKI are affected. To fix it, create a CA with its own key and reissue its certificates; those already issued keep the old URL and are not corrected until reissued."},"2245":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-50","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#cas-that-share-a-key","content":"Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. Until Lamassu rejects imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA — proposed in lamassuiot#834, together with the per-CA CRL that 4.0.0 implements — restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with &#x2A;*Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same SKI collision, so avoid reusing keys across CAs."},"2246":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-51","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"The VA scheduled job checks the CRL settings of every CA on each run and requests a new list when either of these conditions is met:"},"2247":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-52","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"**The list is about to expire:** the remaining validity of `latest_crl` is less than the interval between job runs. This condition always applies and cannot be disabled."},"2248":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-53","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"**The refresh interval has elapsed:** the time since the issuance of the latest published CRL reaches `refresh_interval`. Setting `refresh_interval` to `0` disables only this condition; the list is still regenerated when it is about to expire."},"2249":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-54","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"Setting `refresh_interval` to `0` therefore does not freeze regeneration. With the default 7-day validity and an hourly job, a CRL is renewed up to an hour before it expires even if `refresh_interval` is `0`. Because the job runs on its own schedule, `refresh_interval` is honored only as precisely as the job frequency allows."},"2250":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-55","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"The job frequency is set in Helm with `services.va.job.crl.frequency` (default `0 * * * *`) and accepts a duration (`1h`) or a cron expression. A cron expression is converted into a constant interval equal to the time left until its next firing when the service starts, so it is not aligned with the clock. Keep `validity` well above that interval: with a shorter validity, the list is regenerated on every cycle."},"2251":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-56","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"If generation fails (for example, because the CA cannot be resolved or the signer is invalid), the job logs the warning `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ca-id>` and retries on the next cycle. There is no other renewal path until the cause is fixed, and the last published list keeps being served until it expires. All of this depends on working jobs, storage and signing."},"2252":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-57","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"Settings whose `status` is `ARCHIVED` are skipped: nothing regenerates their CRL."},"2253":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-58","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"With `regenerate_on_revoke`, the event handler requests a new CRL upon revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` to `ACTIVE`. This happens after the status change and has its own failure paths: if generation fails (for example, because of an invalid signer), the event is retried and, once retries are exhausted, moves to the failed-events queue. If the issuing CA has no CRL settings, there is no CRL to regenerate: the event is acknowledged without retry, the VA logs a warning, and nothing appears in the failed-events queue. Confirming revocation does not establish that the new CRL is already published or that all clients have downloaded it."},"2254":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-59","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration","content":"Compare CRL numbers and timestamps before and after a change. Monitor expired lists and generation failures; clients must apply policy when current evidence is unavailable."},"2255":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-60","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-contents","content":"Each entry contains serial number, revocation time and reason. The generator queries certificates in `REVOKED` state for the selected CA. A reactivated certificate disappears when the list is recalculated; an earlier downloaded copy retains its contents."},"2256":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-61","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-contents","content":"The list contains a critical *Issuing Distribution Point* (OID `2.5.29.28`) with configured distribution URLs. Check that your client interprets this extension and that it matches the list’s scope. If rejected, investigate format and scope; ignoring a critical extension is not successful validation."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-62","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"The VA service exposes `GET /crl/{id}` without management authorization. Use the **issuing CA’s** ID, not the end-entity certificate’s ID or serial. Certificates issued before the 4.0.0 upgrade carry `/crl/<ski>` URLs with the SKI of the CA key; that form keeps resolving. First inspect the certificate’s CDP:"},"2258":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-63","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"Replace the following URL with the CDP URL and review exposure in Deployment:"},"2259":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-64","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"You need OpenSSL 3.x and `issuer.pem` containing the direct issuer’s certificate. An accepted signer certificate has the same subject and public key as the CA, so this signature check also works when the settings point to an earlier certificate for that CA and key. Require a valid signature, current timestamps and the expected issuer. For a root that issues directly:"},"2260":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-65","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"`roots.pem` contains deliberately trusted roots. For intermediates, add `-untrusted intermediates.pem` before the `device.crt` argument. `-crl_check` checks the end-entity certificate’s revocation against the local list; it does not download other lists or establish the status of the whole chain. A complete chain check needs the corresponding CRLs and a policy requiring them."},"2261":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-66","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#public-download-endpoint","content":"The test should accept an allowed certificate absent from the list and reject it with `certificate revoked` after publishing and using the list containing its serial."},"2262":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-67","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"The following paths are relative to the VA service; confirm the public prefix in the Gateway. They are not the URLs of the public OCSP and CRL endpoints."},"2263":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-68","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"Method"},"2264":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-69","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"Relative path"},"2265":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-70","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"Operation"},"2266":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-71","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`GET`"},"2267":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-72","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`/v1/crl-settings`"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-73","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"List the CRL settings; filterable at least by `ca_id`, `status` and `legacy_ski`."},"2269":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-74","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`GET`"},"2270":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-75","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`/v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}`"},"2271":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-76","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"Read the `crl_options` and `latest_crl` of a CA."},"2272":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-77","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`PUT`"},"2273":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-78","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"`/v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}`"},"2274":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-79","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"Replace the CRL configuration of a CA."},"2275":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-80","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"You need authentication and CRL settings read or update permissions. Consult your installation’s API reference and Access control. Read current configuration before updating and send the full object expected by the contract — `refresh_interval`, `validity`, `signer_serial_number` and `regenerate_on_revoke`: the backend replaces all four; it does not merge omitted fields. `status`, `legacy_ski` and `latest_crl` are read-only. A `PUT` returns 409 when the settings are not `ACTIVE`, validates the signer only when it changes, and does not regenerate the CRL."},"2276":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-81","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#crl-settings-api","content":"After a change, check saved configuration and the next list actually published. Updating the settings alone does not establish regeneration or client acceptance."},"2277":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-82","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Symptom"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-83","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Cause"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-84","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Action"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-85","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`PUT /v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}` returns 409."},"2281":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-86","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"The settings are not `ACTIVE`: the migration archived them because their CA was deleted before the upgrade."},"2282":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-87","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"There is no API way back to `ACTIVE`. The last CRL keeps being served."},"2283":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-88","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`PUT` returns 400."},"2284":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-89","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"The new signer lacks `cRLSign`, or its subject and public key do not match the CA."},"2285":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-90","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Set the serial number of a valid certificate of that CA."},"2286":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-91","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`PUT` returns 404."},"2287":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-92","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"The CA or the new signer certificate does not exist (for example, the CA was deleted), or the CA has no CRL settings (for example, an imported CA)."},"2288":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-93","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Check the CA ID and the signer serial number."},"2289":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-94","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`PUT` returns 400 from a 3.x client."},"2290":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-95","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`crl_options.subject_key_id_signer` was replaced by `crl_options.signer_serial_number`, which is required."},"2291":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-96","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Update the client to send the new field."},"2292":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-97","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"The CRL does not change after revoking a certificate."},"2293":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-98","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"`regenerate_on_revoke` is `false`, the issuing CA has no CRL settings, or generation failed because of an invalid signer."},"2294":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-99","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Check the VA logs. Missing settings only produce a warning (no retry, no failed-events entry); an invalid signer also leaves the event in the failed-events queue, so fix the signer."},"2295":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-100","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"A verifier rejects the CRL because of the issuer."},"2296":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-101","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"The list was fetched through a legacy `/crl/<ski>` URL and belongs to the CA that owned that SKI when the upgrade ran."},"2297":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-102","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#common-crl-settings-errors","content":"Use the CDP of the certificate being validated: certificates issued in 4.0.0 carry `/crl/<ca-id>` URLs."},"2298":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-103","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#from-the-interface","content":"The CA view lets you inspect and download the available list. Compare version, issuer and timestamps with the copy downloaded from the public endpoint. The CA’s VA settings also let you select the CRL signer: the certificate picker only offers certificates that have a private key and carry the `cRLSign` key usage. If a configured signer’s certificate cannot be resolved, the interface shows `Unknown Certificate (Serial: X)` or `Certificate details unavailable (Serial: X)`. Diagnose differences using PKI troubleshooting."},"2299":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-104","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"RFC 5280: X.509 and CRL profile."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-105","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"OpenSSL: openssl-crl."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-106","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"OpenSSL: openssl-verify."},"2302":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-107","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"<Card title=\"OCSP service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />"},"2303":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl-108","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/crl#references","content":"<Card title=\"Certificate validation\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation\" />"},"2304":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"page","content":"Keys and cryptographic engines","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI","Keys, authorities and certificates"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","content":"Generate, import and operate keys without coupling the PKI to a specific provider."},"2306":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"What the KMS solves"},"2307":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#cryptographic-engines","content":"Cryptographic engines"},"2308":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#file-system","content":"File System"},"2309":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#hashicorp-vault","content":"HashiCorp Vault"},"2310":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#aws-secrets-manager","content":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"2311":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#aws-kms","content":"AWS KMS"},"2312":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#pkcs11","content":"PKCS#11"},"2313":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#azure-key-vault","content":"Azure Key Vault"},"2314":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"Key inventory"},"2315":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#create-or-import-keys","content":"Create or import keys"},"2316":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"Generate a new key pair"},"2317":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"Import an existing pair"},"2318":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#operations-on-a-key","content":"Operations on a key"},"2319":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"View details"},"2320":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-and-verify","content":"Sign and verify"},"2321":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"Sign"},"2322":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"Verify"},"2323":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"Generate a CSR"},"2324":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-locally-with-pkcs11","content":"Sign locally with PKCS#11"},"2325":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#before-deleting-a-key","content":"Before deleting a key"},"2326":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","content":"The Cryptographic Key Management Service (KMS) of Lamassu IoT centralizes the generation, import, custody and use of the platform's keys. This is where you manage the keys that support the CAs and the rest of the signing operations that depend on the PKI."},"2327":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","content":"In the current configuration, Lamassu IoT works exclusively with asymmetric keys, such as RSA and elliptic curves. These keys are used to issue X.509 certificates, sign requests and perform authentication and validation operations within the system."},"2328":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"The KMS unifies key lifecycle management and prevents every service from having to integrate directly with a specific cryptographic provider."},"2329":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"In practice, it lets you:"},"2330":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"Generate new key pairs from Lamassu IoT."},"2331":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"Import keys created externally."},"2332":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"Delegate custody and cryptographic operations to external engines."},"2333":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#what-the-kms-solves","content":"Reuse existing keys for CAs, CSRs and signing or verification tasks."},"2334":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#cryptographic-engines","content":"Lamassu can work with different cryptographic engines depending on deployment requirements. In some environments a software engine is enough; in others custody must be delegated to an HSM, a cloud service or a specialized platform."},"2335":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#cryptographic-engines","content":"A single instance can have several engines configured at the same time, including multiple instances of the same type. This lets you adapt operations to different security, cost and regulatory requirements."},"2336":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#cryptographic-engines","content":"This guide is for the PKI operator: select and use an already enabled engine. Installation, credentials, persistent storage and connectivity belong to Deployment. Before generating a CA, check the default engine, advertised algorithms and a test signature. Availability in your installation depends on its version and configuration."},"2337":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-32","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#file-system","content":"Generates software keys and stores private material in files. Custody depends on installation permissions, storage and backups. Before using it for an operational CA, agree how the key will be recovered and how replicas will access it; the KMS record does not replace those files."},"2338":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-33","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#hashicorp-vault","content":"The adapter uses KV storage for private material. This is not signing within Vault Transit: someone able to read the secret can recover the key. Review read permissions, availability and recovery with the deployment owner."},"2339":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-34","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#hashicorp-vault","content":"The adapter reaches Vault over its configured HTTP(S) endpoint. The Vault server certificate is verified against the system trust store; a Vault signed by a private CA is trusted by adding its CA certificate to the engine's `ca_cert_file` (PEM-encoded). `insecure_skip_verify=true` disables verification and is a last resort; agree the transport settings with the deployment owner."},"2340":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-35","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#aws-secrets-manager","content":"Offers encrypted persistence in AWS at a cost. It protects the key at rest, but an authorized administrator can recover it; it does not provide the same extraction resistance as a KMS or HSM."},"2341":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-36","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#aws-kms","content":"Delegates signing to AWS KMS. The adapter also implements external material import, subject to provider capabilities and permissions. A key generated outside the service has already existed outside its custody; importing it does not remove those copies. Check generation, import and signing as separate operations."},"2342":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-37","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#pkcs11","content":"Allows integrating local HSMs, isolated environments and *Key as a Service* offerings. Generation, persistence and extraction resistance depend on the specific device or provider. It is the most flexible option when policy requires hardware custody."},"2343":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-38","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#azure-key-vault","content":"The backend also provides adapters for **Key Vault Keys** and **Key Vault Secrets**. The former delegates signing to the key service; the latter stores private material as secrets and signs using that material. These are different custody models. Confirm which adapter is enabled in your installation and test the required operations."},"2344":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-39","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#azure-key-vault","content":"Lamassu shows the enabled engines in the console."},"2345":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-40","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#azure-key-vault","content":"Each engine defines which algorithms it supports, such as RSA or ECC/ECDSA, and which key sizes it can generate."},"2346":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-41","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#azure-key-vault","content":"Confirm with the deployment operator which engine is configured as the default. That engine will be used when a process needs to create or persist a key and the user has not selected a specific one, for example when creating or importing a CA."},"2347":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-42","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"The main KMS screen shows the complete inventory of keys registered in the system. It is the reference view for reviewing which keys exist, where they are kept and which entities they relate to."},"2348":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-43","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"The table includes the following fields:"},"2349":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-44","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Name**: descriptive name of the key."},"2350":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-45","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Type**: algorithm and key size, for example RSA 2048 or EC P-256."},"2351":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-46","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Strength**: visual indicator of cryptographic strength."},"2352":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-47","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Public/Private**: whether Lamassu manages the full pair or only the public key."},"2353":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-48","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Crypto Engine**: cryptographic engine keeping the key."},"2354":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-49","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Aliases**: alternative names associated with the key."},"2355":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-50","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Tags**: labels for classification and search."},"2356":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-51","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Related Entities**: certificates or other entities linked to that key."},"2357":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-52","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"From this view you also reach the most common actions:"},"2358":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-53","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**View Details** to inspect metadata, identifiers and relationships."},"2359":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-54","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Generate CSR** to create a PKCS#10 request with the selected key."},"2360":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-55","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Sign / Verify** to test cryptographic operations on the key."},"2361":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-56","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#key-inventory","content":"**Delete Key** to remove the key from the system."},"2362":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-57","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#create-or-import-keys","content":"You need key administration permissions and a reachable engine. The issuance profile must allow the chosen algorithm if the key will be used in a CA or CSR. Open **Create New Key** and choose generation or import."},"2363":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-58","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"If the key will be born inside Lamassu, the recommended flow is to generate a new pair managed directly by one of the configured engines."},"2364":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-59","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"On the wizard's first screen, choose **Generate New Key Pair** and then define the main parameters:"},"2365":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-60","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"**Key Name**: unique and descriptive name."},"2366":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-61","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the key."},"2367":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-62","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"**Key Type** and **Key Size**: algorithm and size or curve."},"2368":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-63","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"**Tags**: metadata to classify the key."},"2369":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-64","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-new-key-pair","content":"**Metadata**: additional information for advanced uses."},"2370":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-65","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"Importing lets you register in Lamassu a key generated outside the platform. It is the usual flow in BYOK scenarios, migrations or integration with cryptographic material already in production."},"2371":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-66","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"When you have an exportable private key and the destination engine supports import."},"2372":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-67","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"When an existing PKI needs to be migrated without reissuing certificates."},"2373":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-68","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"When Lamassu must operate with a root of trust created by a third party."},"2374":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-69","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"In the wizard, choose **Import Existing Key Pair** and complete the requested fields:"},"2375":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-70","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"**Key Name**: descriptive name within Lamassu IoT."},"2376":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-71","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the imported key."},"2377":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-72","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"**Tags**: labels for organization and search."},"2378":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-73","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"**Metadata**: additional optional information."},"2379":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-74","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"**Private Key (PEM)**: private key in PEM format."},"2380":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-75","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#import-an-existing-pair","content":"After a successful import, open the record, check the engine and public key, and perform a test signature. A non-exportable HSM key cannot be uploaded through this PEM form: configure engine access and check how the existing key is registered. Review dependencies and any provider resources already created if an operation fails partway through."},"2381":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-76","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#operations-on-a-key","content":"Each registered key has a detail view and several operational actions."},"2382":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-77","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"The **View Details** screen concentrates the technical and administrative information of the key."},"2383":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-78","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"The **Overview** tab shows, among others, these fields:"},"2384":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-79","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Key Name**: descriptive name."},"2385":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-80","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Key Identifier**: unique system identifier. Lamassu uses PKCS11-based ID formats."},"2386":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-81","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Tags**: associated labels."},"2387":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-82","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Aliases**: alternative names."},"2388":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-83","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Crypto Engine**: engine where the key resides."},"2389":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-84","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"**Algorithm, Key Size & Strength**: algorithm, parameters and strength level."},"2390":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-85","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"The **Related Entities** section shows which Lamassu objects depend on that key."},"2391":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-86","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#view-details","content":"The **Public Key** tab shows public material in PEM. The record’s identifier and URI locate the key; a PKCS#11-formatted URI does not establish that the engine is an HSM or that a local module can access it."},"2392":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-87","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-and-verify","content":"The **Sign / Verify** action validates that the key and the cryptographic engine work correctly."},"2393":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-88","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-and-verify","content":"Sign a test message, save the signature and verify it using the same public key, algorithm and encoding. Then change the message: verification should fail. This tests one operation; it does not establish every engine capability or a CA’s chain."},"2394":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-89","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-and-verify","content":"When you open it, two areas appear: **Sign** and **Verify**."},"2395":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-90","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"This tab uses the private key to generate a digital signature over a message or an already computed *digest*."},"2396":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-91","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"Main fields:"},"2397":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-92","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"**Algorithm**: signing algorithm available for the key type."},"2398":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-93","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"**Message Type**: `Raw` for clear data or `Digest` for a precomputed hash."},"2399":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-94","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"**Payload Encoding**: input encoding, such as UTF-8, Hex or Base64."},"2400":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-95","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"**Message**: content to sign."},"2401":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-96","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign","content":"**Signature**: result of the operation, shown in hexadecimal."},"2402":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-97","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"This tab checks whether a signature corresponds to the associated public key."},"2403":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-98","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"Main fields:"},"2404":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-99","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Algorithm**: must match the algorithm used for the signature."},"2405":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-100","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Message Type**: `Raw` or `Digest`."},"2406":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-101","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Payload Encoding**: encoding of the message."},"2407":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-102","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Message**: original message or hash."},"2408":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-103","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Signature**: signature to check."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-104","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#verify","content":"**Result**: indicator of the validation result."},"2410":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-105","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"The **Generate CSR** action creates a PKCS#10 request signed with the private key kept in Lamassu, without exposing it outside the cryptographic engine."},"2411":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-106","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"This flow is useful for:"},"2412":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-107","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"Requesting certificates from an external CA."},"2413":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-108","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"Renewing an identity while keeping the same key."},"2414":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-109","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"When you open the form, it asks for the subject data and the main certificate attributes:"},"2415":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-110","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Common Name (CN)**: main name of the identity."},"2416":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-111","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Organization (O)**: organization or company."},"2417":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-112","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Organizational Unit (OU)**: department or unit."},"2418":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-113","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Country (C)**: two-letter country code."},"2419":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-114","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**State / Province (ST)**: state or province."},"2420":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-115","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Locality (L)**: city or locality."},"2421":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-116","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Email Address**: contact email."},"2422":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-117","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"**Subject Alternative Names (SANs)**: alternative names or identifiers, such as DNS or IP."},"2423":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-118","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#generate-a-csr","content":"Download the CSR and run `openssl req -in request.csr -noout -verify -text`. Check signature, subject and SAN before sending it. A CSR does not determine the final certificate by itself: issuance policy can replace its fields."},"2424":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-119","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-locally-with-pkcs11","content":"Lamassu also offers specific help for local integrations through **Sign locally with OpenSSL & PKCS11 tools**."},"2425":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-120","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#sign-locally-with-pkcs11","content":"It requires the module, token and permissions of the corresponding provider. Check that the Lamassu identifier matches the object accessible from that module. Module configuration belongs to the client environment or Deployment."},"2426":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-121","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#before-deleting-a-key","content":"Review **Related Entities** and locate CAs, certificates and validation processes that still need signatures. Revoking a CA does not remove the need to publish status during its retention period. Deleting a key does not automatically revoke its certificates either. Plan replacement and retention before using **Delete Key**; provider deletion behavior depends on the engine."},"2427":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management-122","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/key-management#before-deleting-a-key","content":"The KMS refuses to delete a key that certificates still reference, with `409` `key is in use`. Revoking a certificate or letting it expire does not release its key reference. Deleting a CA releases the references from its own current certificate and linked reissued certificates, which stay in place to back the CRL and OCSP, and then attempts to delete the key. If other certificates still reference the key, such as another CA sharing it, the KMS keeps it. The certificates the CA issued keep their own key references, even when revoked. The API lets you delete such a certificate only once its issuer CA no longer exists, and deleting it removes it from the CRL and OCSP responses, so keep revoked certificates for their retention period and delete only expired or superseded ones. Legacy references retained by the upgrade migration are described in the upgrade guide."},"2428":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"page","content":"Online validation with OCSP","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI","Validation"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","content":"Query signed status and verify the responder’s response."},"2430":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"How the responder works in Lamassu"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#embedding-in-issued-certificates","content":"Embedding in issued certificates"},"2432":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"HTTP endpoints"},"2433":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"Generate and verify a query"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#check-from-the-interface","content":"Check from the interface"},"2435":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#references","content":"References"},"2436":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","content":"OCSP queries a certificate’s status and returns a signed response. This guide is for an integrator checking what Lamassu publishes and how the consumer must verify it. For complete identity validation, follow Certificate validation."},"2437":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"The responder retrieves the certificate by serial number and locates its issuing CA in the inventory. It translates persisted status as follows:"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"Record status"},"2439":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"Signed response"},"2440":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"`ACTIVE`"},"2441":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"`Good`."},"2442":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"`REVOKED`"},"2443":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"`Revoked`, with revocation reason and timestamp."},"2444":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"Other states, including `INACTIVE` and `EXPIRED`"},"2445":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"`Unknown` if the certificate and issuer can be retrieved."},"2446":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"An absent serial number causes a lookup error; it does not guarantee a signed `Unknown` response. Lookup or signing errors produce an HTTP failure. Treat that as unavailable evidence according to consumer policy."},"2447":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"The issuing CA itself signs the response through its KMS-associated key. A delegated OCSP signing certificate is not used. An imported CA without a usable key cannot sign responses."},"2448":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#how-the-responder-works-in-lamassu","content":"The implementation sets `thisUpdate` approximately one day before response generation and `nextUpdate` one day after: the declared window is about 48 hours. Inspect received values and apply your freshness policy. The response reflects stored status when queried; a previously cached copy does not change upon revocation."},"2449":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#embedding-in-issued-certificates","content":"The signer constructs OCSP URLs from configured VA domains and adds them to AIA. Without domains, there is no URL to add; an imported certificate retains its original extensions. Inspect the actual certificate:"},"2450":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#embedding-in-issued-certificates","content":"Check that the consumer can reach this URL. DNS, endpoint exposure and domain configuration belong to Deployment. Changing configuration does not modify already signed certificates."},"2451":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"The service accepts:"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"Method"},"2453":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"VA service path"},"2454":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"Format"},"2455":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"`POST`"},"2456":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"`/ocsp`"},"2457":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"DER request, `Content-Type: application/ocsp-request`."},"2458":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"`GET`"},"2459":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"`/ocsp/{ocsp_request}`"},"2460":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"DER request encoded using padded URL Base64."},"2461":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-32","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#http-endpoints","content":"Use POST to avoid client encoding differences. A successful response has `Content-Type: application/ocsp-response` and a DER body. The public prefix depends on the Gateway; use the URL published in the certificate and checked by the operator."},"2462":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-33","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"You need OpenSSL 3.x, `device.crt`, its direct issuer’s certificate `issuer.pem` and the consumer’s trusted roots in `roots.pem`. For an intermediate issuer, also prepare `intermediates.pem` containing the intermediate CAs."},"2463":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-34","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"Generate the request:"},"2464":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-35","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"This example omits the nonce because the responder does not echo it. Verification relies on signature, response correspondence and timestamps; it does not demonstrate freshness through a nonce."},"2465":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-36","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"Replace the example URL with the inspected endpoint:"},"2466":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-37","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"Verify the returned DER:"},"2467":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-38","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#generate-and-verify-a-query","content":"For intermediates, add `-verify_other intermediates.pem`. Require successful response verification and inspect the status corresponding to `device.crt`, issuer, serial, `thisUpdate` and `nextUpdate`. `Response verify OK` validates the response only: also observe `good`, `revoked` or `unknown`. Do not accept an identity based only on the command’s exit code."},"2468":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-39","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#check-from-the-interface","content":"**OCSP Check** shows what Lamassu returns for a selected certificate. Complete the check from the consumer’s network and trust store: a console query does not establish its validation configuration."},"2469":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-40","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#check-from-the-interface","content":"To check a change, save an earlier response, revoke a test certificate and request another response. The new query should reflect persisted status; compare timestamps to rule out reuse of an earlier response. If it fails, follow PKI troubleshooting."},"2470":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-41","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#references","content":"RFC 6960: OCSP."},"2471":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-42","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#references","content":"OpenSSL: openssl-ocsp."},"2472":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-43","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#references","content":"<Card title=\"CRL service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/crl\" />"},"2473":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp-44","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/ocsp#references","content":"<Card title=\"Certificate validation\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation\" />"},"2474":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"page","content":"PKI","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview"},"2475":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","content":"Administer keys and authorities, define issuance and verify the resulting certificates."},"2476":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#start-here","content":"Start here"},"2477":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"What you need"},"2478":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#core-capabilities","content":"Core capabilities"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#build-your-trust-hierarchy","content":"Build your trust hierarchy"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#protect-cryptographic-keys","content":"Protect cryptographic keys"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#issue-and-control-certificates","content":"Issue and control certificates"},"2482":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#publish-validation-status","content":"Publish validation status"},"2483":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"How to check the result"},"2484":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#continue","content":"Continue"},"2485":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","content":"The PKI section explains how to build and operate X.509 trust in Lamassu: which authorities issue, which rules apply and how to check certificates. It is intended for PKI administrators and anyone who needs to use certificates in a consuming system."},"2486":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#start-here","content":"Follow PKI getting started to create a CA, issue a client certificate and verify it with OpenSSL. The result is a certificate, its private key and a verified chain."},"2487":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#start-here","content":"You can complete that path before managing devices. When you need to associate the identity with an inventory or automate its enrollment, continue in IoT Fleets."},"2488":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"An accessible instance and permission to manage the keys, CAs and certificates you will use."},"2489":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"An available cryptographic engine to keep the authority's key."},"2490":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"The certificate's purpose, such as authenticating a client through mTLS."},"2491":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"Consumer requirements: accepted issuers, algorithms, names and permitted usages."},"2492":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#what-you-need","content":"Prepare the instance and cryptographic provider in Deployment. The trust model explains hierarchy choices."},"2493":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#build-your-trust-hierarchy","content":"Authorities sign certificates. A root establishes the trust anchor, and an intermediate separates issuance by environment or purpose. Hierarchy and rotation explains how to maintain trust when replacing an authority."},"2494":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#protect-cryptographic-keys","content":"CA signing keys are managed through KMS and cryptographic engines. A client that generates a CSR can keep its own key: preserve that custody when choosing the issuance method."},"2495":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#issue-and-control-certificates","content":"A profile establishes subject, validity, usage and key restrictions. The CA applies issuance configuration and signs the certificate. In the inventory, you can inspect the result, download it and manage its status."},"2496":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#publish-validation-status","content":"Validation covers the chain, validity, usages and revocation status. Lamassu publishes status through OCSP and CRL; the consumer configures which issuers it recognizes and how it checks that status."},"2497":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"An active certificate in the console is one part of the check. Also verify that:"},"2498":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"its subject and extensions identify the expected client;"},"2499":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"its public key matches the client's private key;"},"2500":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"its chain reaches a root trusted by the consumer;"},"2501":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"its dates and usages allow the operation;"},"2502":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"the consumer applies revocation checks and authorization."},"2503":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#how-to-check-the-result","content":"The issuance path includes the first independent checks."},"2504":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#continue","content":"<Card title=\"PKI getting started\" description=\"Create a CA, then issue and verify a client certificate.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/quickstarts/overview\" />"},"2505":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#continue","content":"<Card title=\"Apply PKI to devices\" description=\"Associate certificates with inventory and prepare enrollment.\" href=\"/docs/platform/iot-fleets/overview\" />"},"2506":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/overview#continue","content":"<Card title=\"PKI diagnostics\" description=\"Check issuance, OCSP and CRL when the result fails.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/troubleshooting\" />"},"2507":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"page","content":"Create your first CA","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI","Getting started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority"},"2508":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","content":"Create an evaluation root and check its certificate before issuing."},"2509":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#before-you-begin","content":"Before you begin"},"2510":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#create-the-authority","content":"Create the authority"},"2511":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#check-the-ca-certificate","content":"Check the CA certificate"},"2512":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#if-the-result-fails","content":"If the result fails"},"2513":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#next-step","content":"Next step"},"2514":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","content":"You will create a root authority with a new key kept by a Lamassu engine. The result is an active CA and its public certificate in `ca.pem`, ready to verify issuance in the next path."},"2515":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#before-you-begin","content":"Console access and permission to create keys and authorities."},"2516":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#before-you-begin","content":"An available cryptographic engine that supports the chosen algorithm."},"2517":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#before-you-begin","content":"OpenSSL 3.x to check the downloaded certificate."},"2518":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#before-you-begin","content":"Use an evaluation instance or an authority intended for these tests. The example creates a root that issues directly; to design an operational hierarchy, see Hierarchy and rotation."},"2519":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#create-the-authority","content":"Follow the interactive walkthrough to create a root CA with a new ECDSA P-384 key, an example subject and a client-certificate issuance profile. The availability and lifetime of profiles depend on your environment; choose values appropriate for your CA."},"2520":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#create-the-authority","content":"The example sets **CA Name** to `Acme Showcase Root CA`, which becomes the subject Common Name (CN). It also sets the optional subject fields &#x2A;*Country (C)** to `ES` and &#x2A;*Organization (O)** to `Acme`; use values appropriate for your authority."},"2521":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#create-the-authority","content":"Open the new CA and select **Certificate PEM**. Download or copy its public certificate to `ca.pem`, including the complete `BEGIN CERTIFICATE` and `END CERTIFICATE` block. The private key remains in the configured engine. To reuse an existing key instead, see the authorities guide."},"2522":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#check-the-ca-certificate","content":"Run from the directory where you saved `ca.pem`:"},"2523":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#check-the-ca-certificate","content":"Check that the subject and issuer match the root you created, the dates include the current time, **Basic Constraints** contains `CA:TRUE` and **Key Usage** allows certificate signing. The last command should return `ca.pem: OK`."},"2524":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#check-the-ca-certificate","content":"You explicitly selected `ca.pem` as the trust anchor for this test. A valid self-signature does not install that trust in other consumers. See the openssl-verify reference for verification options."},"2525":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#if-the-result-fails","content":"If creation fails, check permissions, engine availability and the selected algorithm."},"2526":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#if-the-result-fails","content":"If OpenSSL cannot read the file, copy the complete PEM block again."},"2527":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#if-the-result-fails","content":"If signature or date checks fail, check that you downloaded this CA's certificate and that your computer's clock is correct."},"2528":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#if-the-result-fails","content":"PKI diagnostics covers issuance checks and cryptographic dependencies."},"2529":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/create-certificate-authority#next-step","content":"Continue with Issue and verify a certificate, using this authority and `ca.pem`."},"2530":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"page","content":"Issue and verify a certificate","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Platform","PKI","Getting started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate"},"2531":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-0","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","content":"Issue a client certificate and check content, chain and key correspondence."},"2532":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-1","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"Before you begin"},"2533":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-2","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#start-the-issuance","content":"Start the issuance"},"2534":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-3","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#choose-the-method","content":"Choose the method"},"2535":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-4","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#identify-the-certificate","content":"Identify the certificate"},"2536":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-5","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#configure-key-and-uses","content":"Configure key and uses"},"2537":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-6","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#issue-and-download","content":"Issue and download"},"2538":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-7","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-result","content":"Verify the result"},"2539":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-8","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#inspect-content-and-dates","content":"Inspect content and dates"},"2540":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-9","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-chain","content":"Verify the chain"},"2541":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-10","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#check-that-the-key-matches","content":"Check that the key matches"},"2542":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-11","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#if-the-result-fails","content":"If the result fails"},"2543":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-12","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#next-step","content":"Next step"},"2544":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-13","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","content":"You will issue an end-entity certificate for `device-001` using browser key and CSR generation. The result is `device.crt`, `device.key` and the issuing root's public certificate, `ca.pem`."},"2545":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-14","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"An active root CA with an available signing key and permission to issue. If one does not exist yet, follow Create your first CA."},"2546":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-15","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"Its public certificate in `ca.pem`; this example uses the root created in the previous path."},"2547":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-16","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"OpenSSL 3.x and a working directory with restricted access."},"2548":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-17","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"A client identifier: here we use `device-001`."},"2549":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-18","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#before-you-begin","content":"In this flow, the client's key is generated in the browser and must be downloaded when issuance finishes. If you lose it, you must generate a key and issue another certificate. The certificate inventory lets you download the public certificate, but cannot recover this key."},"2550":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-19","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#start-the-issuance","content":"Open the CA from the previous path, go to **Issued Certificates** and select **Issue New**."},"2551":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-20","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#choose-the-method","content":"Select **Generate Key & CSR in Browser** to follow this example."},"2552":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-21","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#choose-the-method","content":"If the key must be generated and kept on the destination system, use **Upload Existing CSR**, described in the authorities guide."},"2553":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-22","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#identify-the-certificate","content":"Enter `device-001` as the `Common Name`. Add subject details required by your policy and SANs if the consumer uses them to identify the client."},"2554":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-23","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#identify-the-certificate","content":"If you continue with manual registration, the `Device ID` will be exactly `device-001`."},"2555":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-24","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#configure-key-and-uses","content":"Choose an algorithm and size allowed by the profile; for example, ECDSA P-256 if permitted. For this client certificate, select **Digital Signature** and **Client Authentication**, and issue it as an end-entity certificate."},"2556":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-25","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#configure-key-and-uses","content":"Request, for example, 30 days of validity, always within the CA's lifetime. Review the issuance profile if the resulting content differs from the request."},"2557":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-26","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#issue-and-download","content":"Confirm issuance. Save the PEM certificate as `device.crt` and the private key as `device.key` in your prepared directory."},"2558":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-27","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-result","content":"Check in **Issued Certificates** that the certificate appears active. Note its serial number and verify the downloaded material using the checks below."},"2559":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-28","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#inspect-content-and-dates","content":"Check `CN=device-001`, the expected issuer, current validity, **Digital Signature** and the extended usage **TLS Web Client Authentication**. It must be an end-entity certificate: it must not declare `CA:TRUE` or enable **Certificate Sign**. If **Basic Constraints** is present, it must indicate `CA:FALSE`. If you requested SANs, check that the expected identifiers appear in the certificate."},"2560":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-29","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-chain","content":"The expected result is `device.crt: OK`. This check uses your explicit root and evaluates the chain, dates and compatibility with the TLS client purpose. Also check for the expected usages and names through the inspection above."},"2561":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-30","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-chain","content":"If you use an intermediate CA, the trusted root must be in `ca.pem` and you must supply the intermediates with `-untrusted intermediates.pem`. This variant is covered in the trust model."},"2562":{"id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate-31","page_id":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pr-preview/pr-61/platform/pki/quickstarts/issue-certificate#verify-the-chain","content":"The com