cribes that operational context; the resources you create and query are DMSs and devices."],[2134,"Each diagram below is a short sequence of steps you can play, pause or jump between. A CA is drawn as what it is — a key pair — with one row per certificate issued for that key: a root starts with its self-signed certificate and gains a second row when another CA cross-signs it, same subject and same key under a different issuer. Rows a later step will fill are outlined from the start. Solid arrows indicate certificate issuance; dashed arrows indicate configured trust or distribution, as labeled. The green trace is a validation path: it runs from the identity back to the anchor, opposite to the issuance arrows. An available path is not necessarily accepted by a validator."],[1428,"Each DMS participating in the AWS IoT Core integration must have the metadata key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}` configured. The most important field of that configuration is `registration_mode`, which determines how devices are provisioned in AWS."],[2126,"Each domain keeps its anchor; test both directions and restrict unwanted paths."],[2157,"Each domain keeps its anchor. Signatures in both directions allow paths A → bridge → B and B → bridge → A."],[2345,"Each engine defines which algorithms it supports, such as RSA or ECC/ECDSA, and which key sizes it can generate."],[2255,"Each entry contains serial number, revocation time and reason. The generator queries certificates in `REVOKED` state for the selected CA. A reactivated certificate disappears when the list is recalculated; an earlier downloaded copy retains its contents."],[2381,"Each registered key has a detail view and several operational actions."],[656,"Each subscription's filter decides whether it should be notified."],[2049,"Effect of revoking a CA"],[271,"Egress proxy"],[65,"Email"],[69,"Email and chat"],[2431,"Embedding in issued certificates"],[1949,"Enable cryptographic enforcement and list only algorithms and sizes compatib