25,"The backend applies auditing to mutations of:"],[1868,"The backend rejects an import whose certificate lacks the CA basic constraint (`basicConstraints cA flag is not set`), whose serial number matches a stored certificate with different contents, or whose identical certificate already belongs to an existing CA. A deleted CA's certificate record is kept and can be reused when re-importing the same certificate, including its previous status. Re-importing a different certificate with that serial number is rejected; check the certificate and status after re-importing."],[839,"The backend saves **Decommissioned** and attempts to revoke the active version’s certificate with `CessationOfOperation` if the slot was not expired or revoked. It does not traverse all history. Deferred revocation can fail after retirement is saved; check the result by serial."],[1872,"The backend validates before importing any key: a duplicate CA ID and a certificate that is not a CA (missing `basicConstraints` `cA=TRUE`) are rejected, the latter with `400` `certificate is not a CA: basicConstraints cA flag is not set`. If a failure occurs after the key was imported, the key is kept in the KMS and a warning tells you to delete it manually."],[2155,"The benefit is a common trust point without necessarily rebuilding existing PKIs. In exchange, participants must distribute and govern the new anchor, agree policies and assess the impact of its compromise or replacement. A consumer needing only its own domain can continue to trust its existing PCA."],[911,"The blocks are independent:"],[1049,"The body is **MIME multipart/mixed**, with the boundary specified in `Content-Type`. It contains two parts:"],[2129,"The bridge is not a domain anchor and issues end-entity certificates only for its own operation. Agree policies and constraints."],[2101,"The CA hierarchy defines which authorities can sign. A root CA establishes the anchor of trust; intermediate CAs separate operating domains and avoid using 