ertificate and key do not form a matching pair. Enter both values from the same pair; the check runs when the DMS is saved and again on every webhook call."],[334,"The certificate contains the DNS name or IP used by the client."],[2054,"The certificate enters the `ACTIVE` state. Lamassu monitors its `Not After` date; the cryptographic monitoring job marks certificates that have expired as `EXPIRED`."],[2645,"The certificate is already `REVOKED` for a final reason or is `EXPIRED`."],[2653,"The certificate was revoked, or `ca.pem` does not match its issuer."],[2490,"The certificate's purpose, such as authenticating a client through mTLS."],[2661,"The chain validates but the connection fails"],[464,"The chart can enable OpenTelemetry instrumentation for the services that support it, including authz; it does not configure OpenTelemetry for AWS Connector, wfx or the UI. Traces are exported over OTLP HTTP and logs can be directed to a compatible endpoint, such as VictoriaLogs. Use a correlation identifier and the same time window to join Gateway, service and dependency."],[382,"The chart creates Lamassu's `Gateway` and routes. It does not install the Envoy Gateway controller or make the generated `LoadBalancer` Service reachable from your network."],[384,"The chart creates one shared `Gateway` per Helm release. All chart-managed `HTTPRoute` resources attach to it. Envoy Gateway provisions the proxy data plane and a Kubernetes `LoadBalancer` Service for that Gateway."],[220,"The chart defaults to a `GatewayClass` named `eg`. Change `gateway.className` if your platform team uses another name."],[222,"The chart has no Helm dependencies that install PostgreSQL, RabbitMQ, an OIDC provider, Envoy Gateway or cert-manager."],[598,"The chart includes a bootstrap principal that looks for the OIDC role `pki-admin` in `realm_access.roles`. It grants it `SUPER ADMIN` and, when Job Manager is enabled, the administrative policy of its NBI API."],[234,"The chart mounts configured Secrets read-only into every AMQP-consuming workload: the CA certificate under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/ca` and the client certificate and key under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/client`. The mounted file names are always `ca.crt`, `tls.crt` and `tls.key` — the `certificateKey` and `privateKeyKey` values rename only the Secret data keys, never the on-disk paths. The client private key is mounted with restrictive permissions (`defaultMode: 0440`; the CA volume uses `0444`), which works because the chart adds `fsGroup` to the pod security context when `amqp.client.secretName` is set — it uses the container `runAsGroup` (default `65532`) when `fsGroup` is not already set."],[320,"The chart renders a `ClientTrafficPolicy` named `enable-mtls` on its shared Gateway automatically; no Helm value enables or disables it. TLS termination accepts requests with or without a client certificate: devices may present one, but they are not required to. Before forwarding a request, the Gateway removes identity and certificate headers that a client could otherwise supply itself: `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Lms-Source`, `X-Forwarded-Client-Cert`, `X-Forwarded-Cert`, `X-Forwarded-Tls-Client-Cert` (the Traefik spelling), `Ssl-Client-Cert` and `X-Amzn-Mtls-Clientcert`. The Traefik spelling is included because backends that accept `X-Forwarded-Tls-Client-Cert` directly would otherwise receive certificate identity data supplied by the client itself, without a client certificate having been presented. After stripping, Envoy adds its own `X-Forwarded-Client-Cert` carrying the presented certificate and its chain, so services that read certificate identity receive only gateway-generated values. No Helm value changes the list of stripped headers. The Access control page describes how `authz` uses this protection to keep `X-Principal-ID` out of reach of clients."],[421,"The chart's services expose startup, readiness and liveness probes over /health. A pod can be running but receive no traffic if the readiness probe fails."],[1072,"The client requests an identity for its `Device ID` through EST."],[1048,"The code supports RSA and ECDSA, and the `bits` value of the DMS key setting means something different for each. For ECDSA it selects the curve: 224, 256, 384 or 521 map to P-224, P-256, P-384 and P-521, and an unrecognized size falls back to P-256, so explicitly select a compatible size and check the resulting key. For RSA it is used directly as the modulus size; the console offers 2048, 3072 and 4096 bits. Do not reuse an enrolled ID to test this operation without reviewing replacement."],[2562,"The command does not query OCSP or CRL. For revocation checks, continue with Certificate validation. See the openssl-verify reference for its options."],[468,"the commands or queries used;"],[259,"The complete values reference is maintained in `charts/lamassu/VALUES.md` in the Helm repository."],[466,"the confirmed cause;"],[1502,"The connector can write to the AWS IoT Core Device Shadow to send action signals to the device firmware. This feature is enabled per DMS with `shadow_config.enable: true`."],[1509,"The connector creates and attaches the policies configured in the DMS. A typical policy for a device using MQTT and the Device Shadow has this structure:"],[1439,"The connector creates the template, named after the DMS ID, only once. Each later edit of the DMS that keeps the connector key tries to create it again and fails, because the connector does not update existing templates. Later changes to `jitp_config.provisioning_role_arn`, `jitp_config.enable_template` or to the groups and policies the template references must be applied by hand in AWS IoT Core."],[1285,"The connector does not poll Lamassu's services periodically. Instead, it **subscribes to Lamassu's internal event bus** (AMQP or AWS SQS/SNS, configurable) and reacts to the events it cares about: CA creation or update, DMS creation or update, device identity binding, certificate status change, certificate metadata update and devic